---
import Button from '../../../components/Button.astro';
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
import '../../../styles/docs-prose.css';
---

<Layout
  title="Image Trace — Varve Docs"
  description="Prepare, preview, apply, edit, and re-trace raster artwork as editable Varve vectors."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/vector')}>Tools</a> / Image Trace
      </nav>

      <h1>Image Trace</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        Image Trace converts a selected raster image into editable vector paths. It is a bounded
        approximation of source pixels, not a promise to recover the original authoring structure,
        font, hidden shape, or semantic object.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>The workflow</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Select an image.</strong> Use the Object menu's <strong>Vectorize Image</strong>, the image tools inspector, a canvas or Layers context menu, the command palette, or the QuickBar. These entry points open the same Vectorize workflow.</li>
          <li><strong>Choose the intent.</strong> Start with a preset, then choose monochrome, grayscale, colour, or pixel-art. Choose silhouette for filled regions, or centerline for editable strokes on the desktop app.</li>
          <li><strong>Prepare the source.</strong> Open Source preparation for reversible grayscale, contrast, brightness, invert, denoise, fixed threshold, adaptive threshold, alpha cutoff, and border-connected white-background removal.</li>
          <li><strong>Inspect the preview.</strong> Source, Prepared, Overlay, and Vector views use the same trace result. Use Anchors and the Fit/1:1 control to inspect holes, thin gaps, corners, and handles.</li>
          <li><strong>Apply and edit.</strong> Apply re-runs the recipe at the bounded final resolution and inserts ordinary editable paths beside the source in one undoable operation. Use the node editor to refine the result.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Choose a tracing intent</h2>
        <div class="intent-table" role="table" aria-label="Tracing intents">
          <div class="intent-row intent-row--head" role="row">
            <strong role="columnheader">Intent</strong>
            <strong role="columnheader">Use it for</strong>
            <strong role="columnheader">What to expect</strong>
          </div>
          <div class="intent-row" role="row">
            <strong role="cell">Monochrome silhouette</strong>
            <span role="cell">Logos, icons, marks, and black-and-white artwork.</span>
            <span role="cell">Filled contours with compound holes and editable curves.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="intent-row" role="row">
            <strong role="cell">Grayscale</strong>
            <span role="cell">Scans, pencil work, and tonal line art.</span>
            <span role="cell">A bounded palette of filled regions; use preparation to control paper and ink.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="intent-row" role="row">
            <strong role="cell">Colour</strong>
            <span role="cell">Flat-colour illustrations and posterized artwork.</span>
            <span role="cell">Separate fills, a controlled colour count, and cutout or stacked structure.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="intent-row" role="row">
            <strong role="cell">Pixel art</strong>
            <span role="cell">Sprites and grid-aligned artwork.</span>
            <span role="cell">Nearest-neighbour preparation and hard pixel-aligned polygons; no curve smoothing.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="intent-row" role="row">
            <strong role="cell">Centerline</strong>
            <span role="cell">Signatures, diagrams, and genuine single-line drawings.</span>
            <span role="cell">Native desktop strokes with endpoints, branches, loops, pruning, and width; unavailable in web fallbacks.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Source preparation</h2>
        <p>
          Preparation never mutates the imported asset. Begin with the smallest change that solves
          the problem: use adaptive threshold for uneven scans, denoise for isolated paper noise,
          and adjust contrast or brightness only after checking the Prepared view.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Remove white background connected to the image edge</strong> removes only near-white pixels that are connected to the border. Enclosed white counters and white artwork survive.</li>
          <li><strong>Alpha cutoff</strong> controls which partially transparent pixels participate.</li>
          <li><strong>Fixed threshold</strong> uses the trace threshold. <strong>Adaptive threshold</strong> uses a local Bradley–Roth mean for uneven lighting and paper.</li>
          <li><strong>Pixel-art</strong> disables smoothing and background removal by default so the exact-colour/grid promise is not silently changed.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Structure, holes, and seams</h2>
        <p>
          Monochrome output uses compound paths for counters and holes. Colour and grayscale output
          can use either structure control:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Cutout</strong> keeps regions as abutting compound paths with even-odd holes. It is economical, but independently simplified shared edges can reveal hairline seams at high simplification.</li>
          <li><strong>Stacked</strong> paints regions back-to-front. Later regions cover earlier ones, which is usually safer for touching colour regions and transparency-dependent artwork.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          A missing hole, omitted component, or path-budget reduction is reported as a diagnostic;
          it is not silently presented as exact recovery. Raise the maximum path count or lower
          the minimum region area when a small mark matters, then inspect the complexity estimate
          before applying.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Preview, resolution, and diagnostics</h2>
        <p>
          Preview processing is bounded to a long edge of 1024 pixels. Apply can use up to 4096
          pixels, subject to memory and safety limits. The dialog reports the effective trace and
          source dimensions, so a capped run is not described as full source resolution. Matching a
          tolerance value cannot restore detail that was lost through downsampling.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Vector view reports paths, points, holes, omitted holes, complexity, provider, and
          processing progress. The artwork colour is independent of the light, dark, or high
          contrast UI theme. Fit and 1:1 are buttons, so comparison does not depend on a drag-only
          interaction.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Apply, re-trace, and preserve edits</h2>
        <p>
          The source remains available for comparison and the inserted result is a normal editable
          group of paths. Apply creates one meaningful undo step. Preview updates do not write
          authored geometry. Edit Trace restores the saved preparation, tracing options, effective
          provider, resolution, and source identity information.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the source reference changes, Varve warns before a re-trace. A re-trace replaces the
          generated group in place only after the new result succeeds; a failed or cancelled request
          leaves the last valid result intact. Manual divergence should be treated as authored work:
          duplicate or detach the result before applying a recipe that should no longer replace it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section docs-section--note">
        <h2>Current boundaries</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Tracing reads source pixels. Masks, crops, adjustments, effects, and backdrop-dependent visible appearance are not composited into the trace input yet.</li>
          <li>Centerline tracing is native desktop-only; web does not substitute a filled outline.</li>
          <li>Shared-boundary simplification, editable gradient recovery, primitive recognition, and text recognition are not implemented. Text is traced as artwork rather than silently converted to guessed fonts.</li>
          <li>External assets with the same URI but changed bytes are not yet content-addressed by the trace identity hash; review linked sources before re-tracing.</li>
          <li>Standard SVG/PDF export contains the resulting scene vectors. Trace-specific export is not a separate format.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Related reading</h2>
        <p>
          See the <a href={sitePath('/features/image-trace')}>Image Trace feature overview</a> and
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/vector')}>Vector Tools guide</a>. For comparison, Adobe's
          <a href="https://helpx.adobe.com/illustrator/desktop/manage-objects/traces-mockups-symbols/image-trace-panel-options.html">Image Trace options</a>
          and Inkscape's <a href="https://inkscape-manuals.readthedocs.io/en/latest/tracing-an-image.html">tracing guide</a>
          are useful references, not promises about Varve's implementation.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="docs-cta">
        <Button href={sitePath('/features/image-trace')}>See Image Trace</Button>
        <Button variant="secondary" href={sitePath('/download')}>Download Varve</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-page { max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0; }
  .docs-page h1 { max-width: 780px; font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.4rem); line-height: 1.05; font-family: var(--font-editorial); color: var(--text-primary); margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .docs-intro { max-width: 760px; }

  .docs-section p + p { margin-top: 1rem; }
  .docs-section ul, .docs-section ol { padding-left: 1.4rem; }
  .docs-section li + li { margin-top: 0.7rem; }
  .intent-table { overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid var(--border-default); border-radius: var(--website-radius-card); }
  .intent-row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.2fr 1.5fr; gap: 1rem; padding: 1rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default); }
  .intent-row:first-child { border-top: 0; }
  .intent-row--head { background: var(--surface-muted); color: var(--text-primary); font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .intent-row span { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.5; }
  .docs-section--note { padding: 1.25rem 1.25rem 1.25rem 1rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-primary); background: var(--surface-muted); }
  .docs-cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-3); padding-block-start: var(--space-6); }

  .btn-secondary { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; padding: 0.7rem 1.1rem; border: 1px solid var(--border-default); border-radius: var(--website-radius-control); color: var(--text-primary); text-decoration: none; }
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .intent-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.35rem; }
    .intent-row--head { display: none; }
  }
</style>
